You are editing a learner-facing Databricks notebook (`.py` source format)
in a numbered module folder.

Follow these standards, in full, before writing or editing any cell:

Read the module's own design doc first:

- The `README.md` in the same numbered module folder as the notebook (e.g.
  `01 - …/README.md` when editing `01 - …/NN - Title.py`). Use its
  **Notebook navigation** entry for this notebook's number/title as the
  source of truth for topics, subtopics, and must-cover bullets. Do not
  invent sections that are not listed there unless the author explicitly
  asks.

- @docs/standards/notebook-writing.md — notebook structure, cell markers,
  format rules
- @docs/standards/teaching-guidelines.md — pedagogy, explanation style,
  audience assumptions
- @docs/standards/coding-standards.md — PySpark/Python code conventions
- @docs/data/dataset-overview.md — the rideshare dataset's schemas, join
  keys, and file layout; use this as the source of truth for any DataFrame
  or file-read example

Do not duplicate the content of those files here — they are the canonical
source. If something in a notebook conflicts with them, follow the
standards files and the module README's notebook plan.

Never write runtime validation evidence, or update `COURSE_MODULES.md`
status, from within notebook edits — those are author-owned actions
(`docs/validation/` and `COURSE_MODULES.md` respectively).
